  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Blue Roundhead (Stropharia caerulea) is a small to medium-sized mushroom characterized by its distinctive blue to blue-green coloring, particularly on the cap and stem. It belongs to the Strophariaceae family and is commonly found throughout New England during wet periods.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Blue-green coloration",
      "description": "Cap and upper stem display a striking blue to blue-green hue that fades with age or drying",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Partial veil remnants",
      "description": "Young specimens show a delicate ring (annulus) on the stem from the partial veil, though it may disappear with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark purple-brown spore print",
      "description": "Produces a dark purple-brown to nearly black spore print, typical of the genus Stropharia",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Blue Roundhead typically grows on hardwood chips, mulch, disturbed soil, and decaying wood in lawns, gardens, and wooded areas throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The blue pigmentation in Stropharia caerulea may fade significantly when the mushroom dries, making dried specimens appear much less colorful than fresh ones.",
